    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we use the Pythagorean theorem. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. In other words:\n\n\\[ c^2 = a^2 + b^2 \\]\n\nwhere \\( c \\) is the length of the hypotenuse, and \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nIn this problem, we have a right triangle with one side of length 6 cm and another side of length 8 cm. We need to find the length of the remaining side, which is the hypotenuse. Let's denote the hypotenuse by \\( c \\) and the other two sides by \\( a \\) and \\( b \\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[ c^2 = 6^2 + 8^2 \\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the squares of the given sides:\n\n\\[ 6^2 = 36 \\]\n\\[ 8^2 = 64 \\]\n\nNow, we add these squares together:\n\n\\[ c^2 = 36 + 64 = 100 \\]\n\nTo find \\( c \\), we take the square root of both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{100} = 10 \\]\n\nThus, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{10.00} \\]",
